[[compiler-effects]]
=== Effects

[abstract]
An effectful type `<E> T` denotes a suspended computation: a value that,
when run, may perform the effects in `E` and yields a `T`. This section
specifies what the type checker requires of suspensions, when one is run,
and how code generation turns them into closures of no arguments. It also
covers how they are reconciled at every point where a value meets a calling
convention that has no suspensions, so that each computation runs exactly
as often, and in the order, the program says, in every language. The
user-facing description is in <<effects>>.

**A suspension is a value.** `<E> T` and `T` are different types, and no
pass erases, strips or reinterprets the effect layer. The model is
call-by-push-value: a suspension is a thunk, and the only way from `<E> T`
to `T` is to run it. Holding, passing, returning, projecting or storing a
suspension in a record, list or optional runs nothing. Every run runs the
computation again. The compiler never shares, hoists, memoizes, duplicates
or drops a run. This is the one place where morloc's freedom to reorder and
share work stops (<<compiler-sharing>>).

**Effect labels are tags.** The compiler gives a label no operational
meaning. It propagates labels and checks that they are covered. What `IO`
or `Rand` permits is decided by the library that declares it. Effects come
from the signatures of sourced functions. Such a row is the author's claim,
which the compiler takes as given because it cannot look inside foreign
code. Every other row is inferred by composition and checked against the
signature that declares it.

**Typing.** Rows enter the bidirectional checker (<<compiler-types>>)
through subtyping:

[cols="1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Rule | Meaning
| Inclusion | `<E1> T1 <: <E2> T2` when `E1` is a subset of `E2` and `T1 <: T2`. Label order is irrelevant and duplicates collapse. Widening is accepted and narrowing is rejected.
| No coercion | A `T` never fills an `<E> T` slot, and an `<E> T` never fills a `T` slot. `do v` is the only lift, and it has type `<> T`, which fits any row.
| Body fits signature | The effects a definition's body runs must be a subset of the row in its signature. Declaring more than the body runs is legal.
| Layers stay apart | `<E> (<E'> T)` is two suspensions, never `<E,E'> T`.
| Row variables | A row may end in a variable standing for unknown further labels, so combinators can thread a callback's effects into their result. An unconstrained row variable is `<>` at ground.
|===

**Building and running.** A `do` block builds one suspension from its
statements. Only a bind runs a suspension: `x <- t`, or a bare statement
`t`. Application, `let`, projection, passing and returning run nothing.
`!e` is sugar for a bind inserted above the statement that contains it,
with a fresh name in its place. The front end hoists every `!` into an
explicit bind and desugars each block into nested binds, so later passes
see only two operations: *suspend* (build a thunk around an expression)
and *force* (run a thunk once). A force applied directly to a suspend
cancels.

**Evaluation order.** Pure work has no order: the compiler may move,
parallelize or reorder it, including the arguments of one call. The only
order the compiler must keep is the order of effect statements in a `do`
block. A pure `let` in a block is never computed before an effect statement
written above it. A pure argument is evaluated once, at the application.
When a suspension is built from a call whose arguments still need work, the
arguments are computed outside it and the suspension captures their values,
so a second run repeats the call and nothing else.

**Failure is not an effect.** A fallible operation returns `Try e a`, an
ordinary data type. `@throw` abandons the computation and carries no row.
`@try :: <e> a -> <e> (Try Str a)` catches every recoverable failure that
escapes its argument, whichever language raised it, and passes the row
through unchanged. Internal errors and interrupts bypass it. See
<<failure-is-not-an-effect>>.

**Effect boundaries.** After realization and segmentation
(<<compiler-realization>>, <<compiler-calls>>), every position in a
pool's tree sits under a boundary that dictates a calling convention: a
plain value or the suspension itself. A boundary pass compares each
position's declared type with its boundary and inserts a force or a suspend
wherever they differ by a layer:

[cols="2,1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Boundary | Expects | Reconciliation
| Return of a command | plain | The root of a command whose result is `<E> T` is forced once, so the wire carries the `T` the caller asked for.
| Argument of a command | suspension | The caller of a program holds values, not thunks. An argument declared `<E> T` arrives as a `T`, and each reference to it becomes a suspension with a constant result.
| Sourced call | suspension | Foreign functions are eager. When a source is declared to return `<E> T`, the host call becomes the body of a suspension, so running the suspension calls the host function.
| Callback into a host | plain | A morloc function handed to a host at an `A -> <E> C` slot is wrapped as `\a -> force (g a)`, because the host calls it and expects the effect to happen.
| Host function into morloc | suspension | A host function received at that type is wrapped to build the suspension the type promises.
| Cross-pool call, callee side | plain | The callee's entry point runs one layer, and the result travels over the wire.
| Cross-pool call, caller side | suspension | The caller holds the remote call as a suspension, so forcing it makes the call.
| Wire serializer | either | A suspension passed as data crosses as a closure that calls back to its home pool once per run (<<compiler-serialization>>).
|===

Host conversion follows the declared type to any depth: function arguments
(contravariantly), results, record fields, tuple and list elements,
optionals, and packable types. Under a type variable of the source's
signature the host holds the value opaquely, so nothing there is converted.
A sourced `map :: (a -> b) -> f a -> f b` used at `b = <IO> C` builds
suspensions and does not run them. A value that cannot be rebuilt at its
type is refused at build time with an error. After boundaries are placed,
no later pass adds or removes a force. Passes may move and rename code, but
the number of runs on every path is fixed.

**Lowering.** A suspend becomes a closure of no arguments. Its captured
variables are the expression's free variables, copied into the closure when
it is built. A force becomes a call of that closure with no arguments. A
suspension therefore shares every code path a closure already has. It can
be stored in an aggregate, captured, passed to a foreign function as a
callable, and sent across pools (<<compiler-codegen>>). An intrinsic whose
result is declared as a suspension (`@save`, `@try` and the like) is the
eager runtime call. Forcing it calls it, and holding it builds a closure
that makes the call.

[mermaid]
....
flowchart LR
  A["source: <IO> T<br>do / bind / !"] --> B["front end:<br>suspend + force only"]
  B --> C["boundary pass:<br>force/suspend per convention"]
  C --> D["lowering:<br>suspend = closure()<br>force = call()"]
  D --> E["pools: Python, C++, Rust, R<br>same runs, same order"]
....

